{"page": 43, "text": "Three Months Ended September 30, 2026 Compared to the Three Months Ended September 30, 2025 Overview\u2014 Net income was $1,906.0 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to net income of $102.7 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. Our results for the three months ended September 30, 2026 were negatively impacted by special items consisting of costs related to the RBI initiative of approximately $11.0 million, and a loss on extinguishment of debt of $28.0 million, related to the redemption of the 2030 7.875% Senior Notes and the write-off of a portion of unamortized deferred financing costs in connection with refinancing activities. Our results for the three months ended September 30, 2025 were positively impacted by special items consisting of a gain on insurance recoveries of $250.0 million, partially offset by expenses associated with the Martinez refinery fire of approximately $14.6 million, and costs related to the RBI initiative of approximately $7.9 million. Excluding the impact of special items, our results for the three months ended September 30, 2026 reflect a significant increase in refining margins compared to the same period in 2025. The refining margins increase was primarily driven by favorable crack spreads and certain crude oil differentials, as well as higher throughput volumes across the majority of our refineries, partially offset by higher RFS compliance costs and freight and shipping expenses, as well as unfavorable movements on certain crude differentials. Ongoing geopolitical conflicts affecting global supply and trade flows also significantly contributed to these market conditions. 41\n"}
{"page": 44, "text": "Revenues\u2014 Revenues totaled $12.1 billion for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $7.6 billion for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $4.5 billion, or 59.2%. Revenues per barrel were $134.06 and $81.29 for the three months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively, an increase of 64.9% directly related to higher hydrocarbon commodity prices. For the three months ended September 30, 2026, the total throughput rates at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged approximately 293,800 bpd, 156,400 bpd, 180,500 bpd, and 293,100 bpd, respectively. For the three months ended September 30, 2025, the total throughput rates at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged approximately 307,800 bpd, 141,500 bpd, 187,700 bpd and 234,000 bpd, respectively. For the three months ended September 30, 2026, the total barrels sold at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged approximately 321,500 bpd, 167,300 bpd, 172,000 bpd, and 322,500 bpd, respectively. For the three months ended September 30, 2025, the total barrels sold at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged approximately 356,500 bpd, 156,200 bpd, 171,700 bpd and 337,400 bpd, respectively. Total refined product barrels sold were higher than throughput rates, reflecting sales from inventory as well as sales and purchases of refined products outside our refineries. Overall average throughput rates at our refineries were higher for the three months ended September 30, 2026, primarily due to the restart of the Martinez refinery, partially offset by the impact of the planned turnaround of the Martinez hydrocracker complex and Paulsboro crude unit which commenced in September, and unplanned downtime on the East Coast. In the prior-year period, increased maintenance activity and unplanned downtime resulting from the Martinez refinery fire negatively impacted throughput. We plan to continue operating our refineries in line with demand and prevailing market conditions. Consolidated gross margin\u2014 Consolidated gross margin totaled $2,116.6 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $(17.6) million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $2,134.2 million. Gross refining margin totaled $2,937.5 million, or $34.56 per barrel of throughput for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $721.1 million, or $9.00 per barrel of throughput for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $2,216.4 million. During the three months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, our refining margin calculations were not impacted by special items. Consolidated gross margin and gross refining margin increased compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to the persistence of favorable crack spreads and certain crude oil\n"}
{"page": 45, "text": "differentials, which were driven by geopolitical impacts, as well as higher throughput volumes across the majority of our refineries. These favorable impacts were partially offset by higher RFS compliance costs and freight and shipping expenses, as well as unfavorable movements on certain crude differentials. Additionally, our results continue to be impacted by significant costs to comply with the RFS. On March 27, 2026, the Environmental Protection Agency (\u201cEPA\u201d) finalized new RFS requirements for 2026 and 2027, which included a partial reallocation of small refinery exemptions that were granted for years 2023 to 2025 and revised renewable fuel volume requirements for 2026. Total RFS compliance costs were $522.8 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $169.0 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The increase was primarily attributable to the newly finalized RFS requirements for 2026 and 2027, which has resulted in higher RIN prices in 2026 compared to 2025. Average industry margins were notably more favorable during the three months ended September 30, 2026 in comparison to the same period in 2025, primarily due to geopolitical conflicts negatively impacting supply with demand remaining relatively resilient. Favorable movements in benchmark crude differentials typically result in lower crude costs and positively impact our earnings, while reductions in these benchmark crude differentials typically result in higher crude costs and negatively impact our earnings. 42\n"}
{"page": 46, "text": "On the East Coast, the Dated Brent (NYH) 2-1-1 industry crack spread was approximately $64.77 per barrel, or 150.9% higher, in the three months ended September 30, 2026, as compared to $25.82 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Our margins were positively impacted from our refinery specific slate on the East Coast by a widening Dated Brent/Maya differential, which increased by $11.01 per barrel, slightly offset by a weakened WTI/Bakken differential, which decreased by $0.80 per barrel, in comparison to the same period in 2025. The WTI/WCS differential widened to $19.63 per barrel in the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $12.54 in the same period in 2025, which favorably impacted our cost of heavy crude. Across the Mid-Continent, the WTI (Chicago) 4-3-1 industry crack spread was $54.64 per barrel, or 148.3% higher, in the three months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $22.01 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Our margins were negatively impacted from our refinery specific slate in the Mid-Continent by weakened WTI/Syncrude and WTI/Bakken differentials, which decreased by $7.34 and $0.80 per barrel, respectively, in comparison to the same period in 2025. On the Gulf Coast, the LLS (Gulf Coast) 2-1-1 industry crack spread was $69.06 per barrel, or 182.9% higher, in the three months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $24.41 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Margins on the Gulf Coast were positively impacted from our refinery specific slate by an expanded Dated Brent/WTS differential, which averaged a discount of $12.53 per barrel for the three months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to a discount of $4.22 per barrel in the same period of 2025. On the West Coast, the ANS (West Coast - LA) 4-3-1 industry crack spread was $71.45 per barrel, or 143.5% higher, in the three months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $29.34 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Additionally, the ANS (West Coast - SF) 3-2-1 industry crack spread was $73.33 per barrel, or 139.7% higher, in the three months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $30.59 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Our margins on the West Coast were positively impacted from our refinery specific slate by strengthened WTI/ANS differential, which averaged a premium of $1.76 per barrel for the three months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to a premium of $5.01 per barrel in the same period of 2025, and an expanded Dated Brent/WTS differential, which averaged a discount of $12.53 per barrel for the three months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to a discount of $4.22 per barrel in the same period of 2025. Operating expenses\u2014 Operating expenses totaled $662.8 million, or $7.80 per barrel of throughput, for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $589.2 million, or $7.35 per barrel of throughput, for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of $73.6 million, or 12%. The increase in operating expenses compared to the same\n"}
{"page": 47, "text": "period in 2025 was primarily attributable to higher maintenance related to repairs at the Chalmette refinery, as well as increased outside services and employee-related costs, partially offset by lower maintenance at the Martinez refinery following its restart in May 2026. General and administrative expenses\u2014 General and administrative expenses totaled $125.0 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $71.7 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $53.3 million, or 74%. The increase in general and administrative expenses in comparison to the same period in 2025 was primarily due to higher employee-related expenses, including the recognition of incentive compensation, and outside services, including higher legal costs. General and administrative expenses are comprised of personnel, facilities, and other infrastructure costs necessary to support our refineries and related logistics assets. Depreciation and amortization expense\u2014 Depreciation and amortization expense totaled $160.7 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 (including $158.1 million recorded within Cost of sales) compared to $151.6 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025 (including $149.5 million recorded within Cost of sales), representing an increase of approximately $9.1 million. The increase was primarily attributable to an increase in the fixed asset base resulting from capital projects and turnarounds completed since the third quarter of 2025. 43\n"}
{"page": 48, "text": "Gain on insurance recoveries, net\u2014 For the three months ended September 30, 2025, we recognized a gain on insurance recoveries associated with the Martinez refinery fire of $250.0 million. There was no such gain for the three months ended September 30, 2026. Loss on sale of assets\u2014 For the three months ended September 30, 2026, we recognized a loss of $0.7 million primarily related to the sale of non-operating refinery assets. There was no such loss for the three months ended September 30, 2025. Interest expense, net\u2014 Interest expense, net totaled $27.6 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $55.5 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025, a decrease of approximately $27.9 million. The decrease was primarily attributable to lower average outstanding borrowings under our Revolving Credit Facility and the redemption of the 2028 6.00% Notes in June 2026, partially offset by increased interest expense from the issuance of the 2034 7.25% Senior Notes in May 2026. Additionally, interest income increased by $12.3 million during the three months ended September 30, 2026, driven by higher interest rates and higher cash deposits compared with the same period in the prior year. Interest expense for the three months ended September 30, 2026 includes interest on long-term debt, letter of credit fees associated with the purchase of certain crude oils, and the amortization of deferred financing costs. Unrealized loss on 2032 Exchangeable Notes derivatives\u2014 For the three months ended September 30, 2026, we recognized an unrealized loss of $29.9 million on the derivative associated with the 2032 Exchangeable Notes. There was no such loss for the three months ended September 30, 2025. Loss on extinguishment of debt\u2014 For the three months ended September 30, 2026, there was a loss on the extinguishment of debt of $28.0 million related to the redemption of the 2030 7.875% Senior Notes, including the write-off of unamortized deferred financing costs, and the write-off of a portion of unamortized deferred financing costs in connection with the amendment and restatement of the Revolving Credit Agreement. There was no such loss for the three months ended September 30, 2025. Income tax (benefit) expense \u2014 As PBF Holding is a limited liability company treated as a \u201cflow-through\u201d entity for income tax purposes, our Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements generally do not include a benefit or expense for income taxes for the three months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively, apart from the income tax attributable to two subsidiaries acquired in connection with the Chalmette refinery in the fourth quarter of 2015 and PBF Energy Limited (\u201cPBF Ltd.\u201d). These subsidiaries are treated as C-Corporations for income tax purposes. An income tax benefit of $1.8 million was recorded for the three months ended September 30, 2026 in comparison to an income tax expense of $0.8 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. 44\n"}
{"page": 49, "text": "Nine Months Ended September 30, 2026 Compared to the Nine Months Ended September 30, 2025 Overview\u2014 Net income was $3,269.6 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to net loss of $536.2 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. Our results for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 were positively impacted by special items consisting of a gain on insurance recoveries of $356.5 million and a lower of cost or market (\u201cLCM\u201d) inventory adjustment of $313.0 million, partially offset by expenses associated with the Martinez refinery fire of approximately $34.2 million, a loss on extinguishment of debt of $30.2 million related to the redemption of the 2028 6.00% Senior Notes and the 2030 7.875% Senior Notes, as well as the write-off of a portion of unamortized deferred financing costs in connection with refinancing activities, and costs related to the RBI initiative of approximately $29.6 million. Our results for the nine months ended September 30, 2025 were positively impacted by special items consisting of a gain on insurance recoveries of $439.0 million, partially offset by expenses associated with the Martinez refinery fire of approximately $123.1 million, and costs related to the RBI initiative of approximately $21.5 million. Excluding the impact of special items, our results for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 reflect a significant increase in refining margins compared to the same period in 2025. The refining margins increase was primarily driven by favorable crack spreads and certain crude oil differentials, as well as higher throughput volumes and increased barrels sold across the majority of our refineries, partially offset by higher RFS compliance costs and freight and shipping expenses, as well as unfavorable movements in certain crude differentials. Ongoing geopolitical conflicts affecting global supply and trade flows also significantly contributed to these market conditions. Revenues\u2014 Revenues totaled $31.7 billion for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $22.2 billion for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $9.5 billion, or 42.8%. Revenues per barrel were $118.63 and $83.34 for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively, an increase of 42.3% directly related to higher hydrocarbon commodity prices and sale volumes. For the nine months ended September 30, 2026, the total throughput rates at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged approximately 302,400 bpd, 143,800 bpd, 181,000 bpd, and 258,200 bpd, respectively. For the nine months ended September 30, 2025, the total throughput rates at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged approximately 290,100 bpd, 147,000 bpd, 173,200 bpd and 203,700 bpd, respectively. For the nine months ended September 30, 2026, total barrels sold at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged\n"}
{"page": 50, "text": "approximately 333,300 bpd, 155,800 bpd, 168,100 bpd, and 318,300 bpd, respectively. For the nine months ended September 30, 2025, total barrels sold at our East Coast, Mid-Continent, Gulf Coast, and West Coast refineries averaged approximately 335,400 bpd, 155,300 bpd, 165,200 bpd and 318,200 bpd, respectively. Total refined product barrels sold were higher than throughput rates, reflecting sales from inventory as well as sales and purchases of refined products outside our refineries. Overall average throughput rates at our refineries were higher for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, primarily due to the restart of the Martinez refinery, partially offset by the impact of the planned turnarounds, and unplanned downtime at our East Coast and Mid-Continent refineries. In the prior-year period, increased maintenance activity and unplanned downtime resulting from the Martinez refinery fire negatively impacted throughput. We plan to continue operating our refineries in line with demand and prevailing market conditions. 45\n"}
{"page": 51, "text": "Consolidated gross margin\u2014 Consolidated gross margin totaled $3,437.8 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $(607.0) million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $4,044.8 million. Gross refining margin totaled $5,863.8 million, or $24.26 per barrel of throughput for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $1,752.9 million, or $7.89 per barrel of throughput for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $4,110.9 million. Gross refining margin excluding special items totaled $5,550.8 million, or $22.96 per barrel of throughput for the nine months ended September 30, 2026. During the nine months ended September 30, 2025, our refining margin calculations were not impacted by special items. Consolidated gross margin and gross refining margin for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 were positively impacted by the reversal of a prior-year non-cash LCM adjustment of approximately $313.0 million, driven by the increase in crude oil and refined product prices compared to the prior year. Consolidated gross margin, gross refining margin, and gross refining margin excluding special items increased primarily due to the persistence of favorable movements in crack spreads and certain crude oil differentials, which were driven by geopolitical impacts, as well as higher barrels sold at the majority of our refineries. These favorable impacts were partially offset by higher RFS compliance costs and freight and shipping expenses, as well as unfavorable movements in certain crude differentials. Additionally, our results continue to be impacted by significant costs to comply with the RFS. On March 27, 2026, the EPA finalized new RFS requirements for 2026 and 2027, which included a partial reallocation of small refinery exemptions that were granted for years 2023 to 2025 and revised renewable fuel volume requirements for 2026. Total RFS compliance costs were $1,132.1 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $454.0 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The increase was primarily attributable to the newly finalized RFS requirements for 2026 and 2027, which has resulted in higher RIN prices in 2026 compared to 2025. Average industry margins were notably more favorable during the nine months ended September 30, 2026 in comparison to the same period in 2025, primarily due to geopolitical conflicts negatively impacting supply with demand remaining relatively resilient. Favorable movements in benchmark crude differentials typically result in lower crude costs and positively impact our earnings, while reductions in these benchmark crude differentials typically result in higher crude costs and negatively impact our earnings. On the East Coast, the Dated Brent (NYH) 2-1-1 industry crack spread was approximately $45.22 per barrel, or 108.2% higher, in the nine months ended September 30, 2026, as compared to $21.72 per barrel in the\n"}
{"page": 52, "text": "same period in 2025. Our margins were positively impacted from our refinery specific slate on the East Coast by widening WTI/WCS and Dated Brent/Maya differentials which increased by $6.51 and $7.23 per barrel, respectively, in comparison to the same period in 2025, which favorably impacted our cost of heavy crudes. Across the Mid-Continent, the WTI (Chicago) 4-3-1 industry crack spread was $39.82 per barrel, or 109.2% higher, in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $19.03 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Our margins were negatively impacted from our refinery specific slate in the Mid-Continent by a weakened WTI/Syncrude differential, which decreased by $3.45 per barrel, in comparison to the same period in 2025. On the Gulf Coast, the LLS (Gulf Coast) 2-1-1 industry crack spread was $49.54 per barrel, or 139.3% higher, in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $20.70 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Margins on the Gulf Coast were positively impacted from our refinery specific slate by an expanded Dated Brent/WTS differential, which averaged a discount of $11.94 per barrel for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to a discount of $4.04 per barrel in the same period of 2025. 46\n"}
{"page": 53, "text": "On the West Coast, the ANS (West Coast) 4-3-1 industry crack spread was $54.07 per barrel, or 99.2% higher, in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $27.14 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Additionally, the ANS (West Coast) 3-2-1 industry crack spread was $57.77 per barrel, or 87.8% higher, in the nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to $30.76 per barrel in the same period in 2025. Our margins on the West Coast were negatively impacted from our refinery specific slate by a weakened WTI/ANS differential, which averaged a premium of $5.47 per barrel for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to a premium of $4.80 per barrel in the same period of 2025, slightly offset by an expanded Dated Brent/WTS differential, which averaged a discount of $11.94 per barrel for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to a discount of $4.04 per barrel in the same period of 2025. Operating expenses\u2014 Operating expenses totaled $1,970.0 million, or $8.15 per barrel of throughput, for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $1,903.0 million, or $8.56 per barrel of throughput, for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of $67.0 million, or 3.5%. The increase in operating expenses compared to the same period in 2025 was primarily attributable to higher insurance, outside services and employee-related costs, partially offset by reduced natural gas costs and lower maintenance expenses, primarily at the Martinez refinery, following its restart in May 2026. General and administrative expenses\u2014 General and administrative expenses totaled $358.3 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $216.9 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, an increase of approximately $141.4 million or 65.2%. The increase in general and administrative expenses in comparison to the same period in 2025 was primarily due to higher employee-related expenses, including the recognition of incentive compensation, and outside services, including higher legal costs, and costs incurred in connection with the RBI initiative. General and administrative expenses are comprised of personnel, facilities, and other infrastructure costs necessary to support our refineries and related logistics assets. Depreciation and amortization expense\u2014 Depreciation and amortization expense totaled $463.0 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 (including $456.0 million recorded within Cost of sales) compared to $463.2 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025 (including $456.9 million recorded within Cost of sales), representing a decrease of approximately $0.2 million. The decrease was primarily attributable to certain catalyst and turnaround costs that became fully amortized in 2025, partially offset by an increase in the fixed asset base resulting from capital projects and turnarounds completed since the third quarter of 2025. Gain on insurance recoveries, net\u2014 For the nine\n"}
{"page": 54, "text": "months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, we recognized gains on insurance recoveries associated with the Martinez refinery fire of $356.5 million and $439.0 million, respectively. Loss (gain) on sale of assets\u2014 For the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, we recognized a loss of $1.0 million and a gain of $0.2 million, respectively, primarily related to the sale of non-operating refinery assets. Interest expense, net\u2014 Interest expense, net totaled $112.6 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $145.4 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025, a decrease of approximately $32.8 million. The net decrease was primarily attributable to increased capitalized interest related to the Martinez refinery rebuild, lower average outstanding borrowings under our Revolving Credit Facility, and the redemption of the 2028 6.00% Senior Notes in June 2026, partially offset by increased interest expense from the issuance of the 2034 7.25% Senior Notes in May 2026. The lower principal balance of the 2034 7.25% Senior Notes more than offset the higher interest rate compared to the 2028 6.00% Senior Notes. Interest expense also reflects the issuance of the 2030 9.875% Senior Notes in March 2025. Additionally, interest income increased by $15.3 million during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, driven by higher interest rates and higher cash deposits compared with the same period in the prior year. Interest expense for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 includes interest on long-term debt, letter of credit fees associated with the purchase of certain crude oils, and the amortization of deferred financing costs. 47\n"}
{"page": 55, "text": "Unrealized loss on 2032 Exchangeable Notes derivatives\u2014 For the nine months ended September 30, 2026, we recognized an unrealized loss of $29.9 million on the derivative associated with the 2032 Exchangeable Notes. There was no such loss during the nine months ended September 30, 2025. Loss on extinguishment of debt\u2014 For the nine months ended September 30, 2026, there was a loss on the extinguishment of debt of $30.2 million, related to the redemption of the 2028 6.00% Senior Notes and 2030 7.875% Senior Notes, including the write-off of unamortized deferred financing costs, as well as the write-off of a portion of unamortized deferred financing costs in connection with the amendment and restatement of the Revolving Credit Agreement. There was no such loss during the nine months ended September 30, 2025. Income tax (benefit) expense \u2014 As PBF Holding is a limited liability company treated as a \u201cflow-through\u201d entity for income tax purposes, our Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements generally do not include a benefit or expense for income taxes for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively, apart from the income tax attributable to two subsidiaries acquired in connection with the Chalmette refinery in the fourth quarter of 2015 and PBF Ltd. These subsidiaries are treated as C-Corporations for income tax purposes. An income tax benefit of $10.6 million was recorded for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 in comparison to an income tax expense of $0.8 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025.\n48\n"}
{"page": 56, "text": "Non-GAAP Financial Measures Management uses certain financial measures to evaluate our operating performance that are calculated and presented on the basis of methodologies other than in accordance with GAAP (\u201cNon-GAAP\u201d). These measures should not be considered a substitute for, or superior to, measures of financial performance prepared in accordance with accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America (\u201cGAAP\u201d), and our calculations thereof may not be comparable to similarly entitled measures reported by other companies. Special Items The Non-GAAP financial measures presented include Adjusted fully-converted net income (loss) excluding special items, gross refining margin excluding special items, EBITDA excluding special items, and net debt to capitalization ratio excluding special items. Special items for the periods presented relate to LCM inventory adjustments, expenses associated with the Martinez refinery fire, gain on insurance recoveries, costs related to the RBI initiative, and loss on extinguishment of debt. See \u201cNotes to Non-GAAP Financial Measures\u201d below for more details on all special items disclosed. Although we believe that Non-GAAP financial measures, excluding the impact of special items, provide useful supplemental information to investors regarding the results and performance of our business and allow for helpful period-over-period comparisons, such Non-GAAP financial measures should only be considered as a supplement to, and not as a substitute for, or superior to, the financial measures prepared in accordance with GAAP. Gross Refining Margin and Gross Refining Margin Excluding Special Items Gross refining margin is defined as consolidated gross margin excluding refining depreciation and operating expenses. We believe both gross refining margin and gross refining margin excluding special items are important measures of operating performance and provide useful information to investors because they are helpful metric comparisons to the industry refining margin benchmarks, as the refining margin benchmarks do not include a charge for refining operating expenses and depreciation. In order to assess our operating performance, we compare our gross refining margin (revenues less cost of products and other) to industry refining margin benchmarks and crude oil prices as defined in the table below. Neither gross refining margin nor gross refining margin excluding special items should be considered an alternative to consolidated gross margin, income from operations, net cash flows from operating activities or any other measure of financial performance or liquidity presented in accordance with GAAP. Gross refining margin and gross refining margin excluding special items presented by other companies may not be comparable to our presentation, since each company may define these terms differently. 49\n"}
