{"page": 81, "text": "Item 1A. Risk Factors The following risk factors supplement and/or update the risk factors previously disclosed in our Annual Report on Form 10-K for the year ended December 31, 2025: The accounting method for the 2032 Exchangeable Notes could adversely affect PBF Energy\u2019s and the issuers\u2019 financial condition and consolidated financial results. The accounting method for reflecting the 2032 Exchangeable Notes on our Condensed Consolidated Balance Sheets, recognizing amortized interest expense, and reflecting the underlying shares of common stock in diluted earnings per share could adversely affect our reported earnings, financial condition, and liquidity. In accordance with GAAP, the 2032 Exchangeable Notes are reflected as a liability on our Condensed Consolidated Balance Sheets, with an initial carrying amount equal to the principal amount of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes, net of issuance costs. The issuance costs are treated as a debt discount for accounting purposes, and amortized into interest expense over the term of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes. As a result of this amortization, the interest expense recognized for accounting purposes will be greater than the cash interest payments on the 2032 Exchangeable Notes, which will result in lower reported income. Furthermore, if any of the conditions to the exchangeability of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes are satisfied, then we (and the issuers) may be required under GAAP to reclassify the liability carrying value of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes as a current, rather than a long-term, liability. This reclassification could be required even if no noteholders exchange their 2032 Exchangeable Notes, and could materially reduce our (and the issuers\u2019) reported consolidated working capital. We are subject to risks related to the capped call transactions. In connection with the pricing of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes, PBF Holding and PBF Holding\u2019s wholly-owned subsidiary, PBF Finance Corporation (together with PBF Holding, the \u201cIssuers\u201d) and PBF Energy entered into capped call transactions with certain of the initial purchasers of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes or their respective affiliates and certain other financial institutions (the \u201cOption Counterparties\u201d). The capped call transactions cover, subject to anti-dilution adjustments substantially similar to those applicable to the 2032 Exchangeable Notes, the number of shares of PBF Energy\u2019s Class A common stock initially underlying the 2032 Exchangeable Notes, and are intended generally to reduce potential dilution to the PBF Energy\u2019s Class A common stock upon exchange of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes and/or offset certain cash payments the Issuers may be required to make in excess of the principal amount of exchanged 2032 Exchangeable Notes, in each case subject to a cap. The Option Counterparties are, or are affiliates of, financial institutions, and we are subject to the risk that they might default under the capped call transactions. Our exposure to the credit\n"}
{"page": 82, "text": "risk of the Option Counterparties will not be secured by any collateral. Global economic conditions have from time to time resulted in the actual or perceived failure or financial difficulties of many financial institutions. If an Option Counterparty becomes subject to insolvency proceedings, then we would become unsecured creditors in those proceedings with a claim equal to our exposure at that time under our transactions with that option counterparty. Our exposure will depend on many factors, and can provide no assurances as to the financial stability or viability of the Option Counterparties. In addition, upon a default by an Option Counterparty, PBF Energy may suffer adverse tax consequences and there may be more dilution than currently anticipated with respect to its Class A common stock. 66\nThe capped call transactions are complex, and they may not operate as planned. For example, the terms of the capped call transactions may be subject to adjustment, modification or, in some cases, renegotiation if certain corporate or other transactions occur. Accordingly, these transactions may not operate as we intend if the terms of the capped call transactions are adjusted as a result of transactions in the future or upon unanticipated developments that may adversely affect the functioning of the capped call transactions. In addition, the option counterparties and/or their respective affiliates may modify their hedge positions by entering into or unwinding various derivatives with respect to PBF Energy\u2019s Class A common stock and/or purchasing or selling PBF Energy\u2019s Class A common stock in secondary market transactions prior to the maturity of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes (and are likely to do so (x) during any observation period related to an exchange of 2032 Exchangeable Notes, following any redemption of 2032 Exchangeable Notes by the Issuers or following any repurchase of 2032 Exchangeable Notes by the Issuers in connection with any fundamental change and (y) following any repurchase of the 2032 Exchangeable Notes by the Issuers other than in connection with any such redemption or any fundamental change if the Issuers elect to unwind a corresponding portion of the capped call transactions in connection with such repurchase). This activity could cause or avoid an increase or a decrease in the market price of PBF Energy\u2019s Class A common stock.\n67\nItem 6. Exhibits The exhibits listed in the accompanying Exhibit Index are filed or incorporated by reference as part of this report and such Exhibit Index is incorporated herein by reference. EXHIBIT INDEX\nExhibit\nNumber Description\n22.1* List of Guarantor Subsidiaries.\n"}
